This might suggest that Older articles have more negative sentiment and less positive. It is important to note that older articles also have the most extensive coverage, 54 articles, and 51 were published in 2021 alone. This coincides with global interest in the Chinese re-education camps and repression towards the indigenous population of Xinjiang. 

Part 3 of the assignment divides the articles into sentences to provide sentiment analysis, though with major flaws. This sentiment analysis missed the specifics of certain sentences and marked them as neutral or positive instead. For example, a sentence, "Her daughters begged her to stay, Abbas said in an interview." is marked as neutral (.000), just like the phrase "Six days later they took my own sister.", which has clearly negative connotations that imply kidnapping. The "neutral" phrase is also this one: "Experts estimate more than a million members of ethnic minorities have been put in internment camps since the campaign began." A sentence such as "Her advocacy is at the cost of my own sister's freedom, so it is very personal to me." is marked as positive and given +.310 points. Another "positive" phrase in a different article is this: "She is serving a sentence with all legitimate rights guaranteed." and this: "Thirty-nine countries including Britain, the United States and Germany last week condemned China for its Xinjiang policies." Sentiment analysis may be a great tool for analyzing large volumes of articles, but if you look closely, it may not account for context and other nuances that are crucial in such text analysis. Human oversight is always needed, and probably another more advanced sentimentr package.  

In part 4, I examined the profanity with the highest score (most negative): article 6, followed by article 44. The emotion score from the lexicon package has shown that the most frequent "emotions" are "trust" and "fear," which are linked to words such as "association" and "law" for the former, and "government", "forced," and "birth" for the latter. The word "birth" also had another emotion: "anticipation." 